not watching movies that i  understood easily in my own language not  being with friends and family but  sitting there trying to  get my brain cells to get used to this  new language  and  sometimes and i can remember in hong  kong that when i would come out from  that and i would just communicate with  people  sometimes i felt a certain awkwardness  that the uh  this sort of intensive  interaction with a strange language  forcing the brain to get used to new  language patterns  can  you know create a sense of loneliness  you are alone with something that you  don't understand very well and like even  now for example you know my wife might  be watching  a movie on netflix in english or french  language where which we understand and  where in any case there are subtitles  for the french and it would be nice to  just sit there and enjoy the movie but  i at some level i want to work on my  arabic or my persian and so i go  downstairs by myself  and i work on these  uh whatever they might be  podcasts or  other you know lessons in our link  library where i'm  saving words and phrases and gradually  trying to get used to the language i  wander around the house with my airport  airpods in my ears  so that i can take advantage of whatever  time i have that i'm cleaning up or  something to get more of the language in  me i want to sort of force a change of  transformation of my brain so that i can  easily deal with this language  that i'm convinced put some kind of a  psychological pressure on us  and it can feel lonely at times  and so there are these ways in which  sort of intensive language learning  affects us  now that's no different than any other  pursuit that we go at sort of  intensively and there was a movie called  the loneliness of the long distance  runner i don't know much about the movie  i gather it had to do with social  classes in england  but anything you do you know with  intensity whether it be as a piano  player or as uh  you know a performance athlete or  whether you're pursuing you know science  or  whether it's  you as an individual  sort of striving to acquire knowledge or  skills in a particular field and you're  shutting yourself off from other people  that  can create a sense of loneliness now  there are many ways that we can you know  interact with other people whether we're  in science class or whether we have  teammates  or  language class or the people i talk to  online and i cherish the online  sessions that i have i was with  adele sammy of egypt this morning and  he's taking me through egyptian arabic  and those are wonderful moments and  those are social moments but there's  also a lot of time when it's it's lonely  it's just the learner and the language  and i just want to mention that because  it's something people should be aware of  they shouldn't consider it strange that  at times they feel lonely in their  pursuit of the language and it's  important to remember that ultimately  the reward is great  so i now communicate comfortably in  chinese japanese french russian  languages that i learned a while ago  and i  even finding now that i'm able to  communicate fairly comfortably in  persian with with sacra  persian being easier than arabic  now i've completely confused myself in  arabic because i've started into  egyptian arabic which is fun because  it's a it's a country where they speak  the language egypt is whereas there is  no country that speaks standard arabic  but it has kind of caused both of them  to get further confused  and so i've got to struggle with that  and i'm struggling with it you know on  my own so there is a certain loneliness  attached to language learning  until we get to a stage where we are  comfortable communicating and we kind of  rejoin the world around us so just a  thought that i had that i wanted to  share with you  bye for now
 

